#limitation(title: [Critical Caveats on the Mild / Lifetime-Energy Recognition Proposal])[
The case for integrating a mild/lifetime-energy presentation into ME/CFS recognition rests on evidence that is real but bounded. Four concerns must stay visible alongside the proposal @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue @Harvey2009neurasthenia:

*1. Risk of over-broadening the syndrome.* Removing the "not lifelong" exclusion could capture tired-but-well people with no PEM, inflating ME/CFS prevalence and diluting the syndrome's specificity. The counter-guard is to make PEM, not baseline energy, the gate — but PEM is the very feature most susceptible to self-report bias at the mild end.

*2. PEM as a discriminator may not hold at the mild end.* Mild or very-early cases may lack a detectable, reportable PEM signature, so a PEM-gated recognition pathway could still miss the population it aims to capture.

*3. The premorbid signal may be high, not low, energy.* Population data show the clearest predictor of later persistent fatigue is *excessive* childhood energy, not low baseline energy @Harvey2009neurasthenia. If this null holds, lifelong low energy is a red herring, and the low-baseline framing survives only as a recognition/access gap, not a predisposing biology.

*4. Evidence-quality limits.* The strongest new finding (Sunnquist) derives from a single cohort; 3 of the 6 Phase-1 papers share the DePaul/Jason group; no longitudinal study tracks mild/lifetime-energy individuals into moderate/severe ME/CFS; and severity is self-reported, not measured @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue @Jason2010FatigueTypes @Brown2013EnergyEnvelope @Collin2015childFatigue.

*(Severity applicability: mild / lifetime-energy.)*

*Consequence:* These caveats mean the mild/lifetime recognition proposal should be treated as a testable hypothesis and a recognition pathway, not an established diagnostic expansion — over-application could do more harm than the under-recognition it tries to fix.

*(Origin: brainstorm — critical categories 10–12.)*
] <lim:mild-lifetime-critical-caveats>
